---
import { Code } from "astro:components"

import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro"
import Card from "../components/Card.astro"

import defaultConfig from "../../../default.lua?raw"
---

<Layout title="Home">
  <Card>
    <i>boring</i> is a minimalist, non-monolithic Wayland compositor built on
    <a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots">wlroots</a> that is designed
    to be hackable and extensible, reminiscent of <code>dwm</code> and <code
      >dwl</code
    >. Functionality is provided through plugins; we give you the tools and
    leave you to play.
  </Card>
  <section>
    <h1>Overview</h1>
    <p>
      Unlike most compositors, <i>boring</i> implements only the bare necessities
      of a compositor. Window management (<i>like tiling and stacking layouts</i
      >), decorations (<i>like borders and shadows</i>), and desktop components
      (<i>like wallpapers</i>) are provided through <i>plugins</i> loadable at runtime
      instead of the compositor itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      This doesn't mean that <i>boring</i> is featureless or incomplete, however.
      An extensive collection of default plugins is available to provide you with
      most of the functionality you should need if you are not technologically inclined.
      Unlike many other minimal compositors, we also have a configuration file system
      to easily tweak the compositor and its plugins to your liking.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h1>Implementation</h1>
    <p>
      <i>boring</i> is written in Lua. It specifically makes use of <a
        href="https://luajit.org/luajit.html">LuaJIT</a
      > for its performance and its binding capabilities. No native code is used at
      any point, other than in the form of the libraries the compositor depends on,
      such as
      <a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots">wlroots</a>.
      Plugins and configuration files are also written in Lua; they have access
      to the whole of the compositor's API, so plugins shouldn't be too limited.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most Wayland protocols are supported. We make use of wlroots wherever
      possible, but protocols unsupported by wlroots will be provided by the
      compositor itself. You can list which protocols we report for by invoking
      the compositor with the <code>--protocols</code> flag.
    </p>
    <h2>Uplifting</h2>
    <p>
      There is a perpetual uplift effort to reimplement core Wayland protocols
      directly in the compositor to reduce our dependency on wlroots. This is
      not a criticism of wlroots (it is an <i>excellent</i> library), but rather a
      desire for greater control over the implementation of the protocols we use and
      hopefully, one day, the elimination of all libraries not written in Lua. Any
      contributions to this effort are welcome.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h1>Configuration</h1>
    <p>
      Configuration files in <i>boring</i> are essentially headless plugins. They
      have the same environment as plugins, but unlike plugins, they are reloaded
      automatically when changes are detected and cannot be unmounted without physically
      removing the files from the filesystem.
    </p>
    <p>
      The configuration file is located at <code
        >~/.config/boring/config.lua</code
      >. You can write additional configuration files and include them in your
      main configuration file using <code>require</code>. If the configuration
      file is missing, the default configuration below will be used.
    </p>
    <h2>Default configuration</h2>
    <Code code={defaultConfig} lang="lua" theme="gruvbox-light-soft" />
  </section>
</Layout>
